<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ternate Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1257 CE – 1914 CE (approx.) · capital Ternate</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The clove sultanate of the North Moluccas, which drove out the Portuguese in 1575 and dominated the eastern spice islands for a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ternate, a small volcanic island off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halmahera">Halmahera</a>, was one of the few places in the world where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> grew. Its kings, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moloku-kie-raha">Moloku Kie Raha</a> alliance with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore</a>, Bacan and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jailolo-sultanate">Jailolo</a>, grew rich supplying Javanese and Malay traders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zainal-abidin-of-ternate">Sultan Zainal Abidin</a>, who had studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> at Giri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, formally adopted Islam in the late 15th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Portuguese built a fort on Ternate in 1522. In 1570 they murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hairun">Hairun</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babullah">Babullah</a> besieged the fort until the Portuguese left in 1575. Babullah then claimed authority over 72 islands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-papua">Papua</a>, and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> in 1579. Ternate's power rested on clove revenues and war fleets of kora-kora <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1607 Ternate allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, but gradually became its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, accepting the destruction of clove trees outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> deposed and exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Muhammad Usman in 1914 after unrest. The sultanate survives today as a cultural institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-maluku">North Maluku</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ternate-sultanate.jpg" alt="Ternate Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Kailanmapper · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Ternate_Sultanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1257 CE – 1914 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ternate</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zainal Abidin, Hairun, Babullah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ternate, Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Indonesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Ternate, Ternate</dd></dl></aside></div>

